Rivet bundles C++, C, Python, Rust, and SwiftPM providers. The editor requires Neovim 0.11 or newer. The optional CLI uses Lua or LuaJIT; the process and filesystem adapter targets macOS and Linux.

## Language modes

| Choice | Project interfaces | Standard library |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C++17 | Headers and source files | Headers |
| C++20 | Headers, single-file modules, or split modules | Headers |
| C++23 | Headers, single-file modules, or split modules | Headers, or opt-in `import std` with a supported toolchain |
| C++26 | Headers, single-file modules, or split modules | Headers, or opt-in `import std` with a supported toolchain |

C++26 remains the default. Rivet sets `CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED YES` and disables language extensions; it does not silently lower the standard. Accepting a language mode does not establish support for every feature of that edition. C++11 and C++14 are not offered yet.

A new C++17 project defaults to headers. Requesting modules explicitly with C++17, or `import std` with C++20, is rejected before creation. Existing manifests without `module_layout` retain the split arrangement; no source migration is performed.

## Tested combinations

The local integration suite exercises generated projects with Homebrew LLVM 23.1.2, CMake 4.4.4, and Ninja on macOS:

- Headers build and pass CTest in C++17/20/23/26.
- Named modules build and pass CTest in C++20/23/26, with both single-file and split starters.
- C++23 `import std` is exercised with a single-file module; the C++26 cases cover project headers and named modules.
- Custom preset includes, conditions, environments, local cache overrides, executable artifact discovery, and clangd database selection have integration coverage.

Lua 5.5 and LuaJIT exercise the shared model; Neovim tests cover wizard navigation, review/cancellation, headers, remembered tasks, and asynchronous diagnostics. Linux was also tested in an Ubuntu 26.04.1 ARM64 container with Lua 5.4.8, LuaJIT, Neovim 0.11.6, LLVM/libc++ 21.1.8, Ninja, and CMake 4.4.4. That run covers headers in C++17/20/23/26, named modules in C++20/23/26, and C++23/26 `import std`, including reconfiguration and added sources. Other distributions, compiler combinations, and Linux GPU runtimes still need their own validation.

CI repeats the Lua, Neovim, and integration suites on macOS with Homebrew LLVM and on Ubuntu with LLVM/libc++ 21. GCC is not exercised: generated projects may build with it in header mode, but named modules and `import std` with GCC are untested and unsupported for now.

## Toolchain requirements

Generated C/C++ projects require CMake 3.30 or newer. Module creation uses Ninja and requires compatible compiler dependency scanning; Ninja must be 1.11 or newer. Conventional headers can also use Unix Makefiles.

`import std` has additional restrictions: Rivet's experimental CMake gate is verified for **4.4.0 through 4.4.4**, and the compiler/library must advertise the selected 23 or 26 mode. This gate is version-specific. The [module guide](/docs/modules/) explains metadata discovery and editor setup.

CMake evaluates custom preset validity and conditions. Rivet requires an explicit build directory inside the project and rejects symlink paths and in-source builds. Native build/test settings are respected. Multi-config executable runs require a build preset that identifies the configuration. Standalone CMake remains available for workflows outside these bounds.

## Moving to Linux

Install Neovim 0.11+ and the tools for the languages you use. The plugin uses Neovim's bundled Lua; the optional CLI additionally needs Lua or LuaJIT. Ubuntu's LLVM tooling is split into compiler, scanner, formatter, and language-server packages, so check for `clang++`, `clang-scan-deps`, `clang-format`, and `clangd` independently. Run `:RivetDoctor` in each actual project.

For Clang `import std`, use matching libc++ and libc++abi development packages and select libc++ explicitly in your local configure preset with `CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS: "-stdlib=libc++"`. The tested Ubuntu combination used the 21-series packages. It also required CMake 4.4.4 instead of Ubuntu's packaged CMake 4.2.3. Conventional headers and named project modules do not require that import-std gate or a switch to libc++.

Projects stay portable by keeping machine-specific compiler and SDK paths in `CMakeUserPresets.json` and `.rivet.local.json`. Recreate those local choices on Linux instead of copying macOS Homebrew paths. Metal and Xcode workflows remain macOS-specific; use Vulkan, OpenGL, or a suitable WebGPU backend on Linux. Linux SwiftPM does not supply Apple's SwiftUI or Metal frameworks.

## Additional local verification

| Workflow | Local result |
| --- | --- |
| C17, mixed C/C++ | Build/run/CTest and ABI header coverage |
| CPU / OpenMP | Compiled and executed correctness tests |
| MPI / CUDA | Generation and option validation covered; runtime unverified without MPI/NVIDIA tooling |
| Metal-cpp | Compiled MSL and C++; GPU render/readback passed |
| Native WebGPU / Dawn | Compiled C++/WGSL; GPU render/readback passed |
| OpenGL / GLFW 3.4 | Compiled, loaded core functions, rendered and checked a pixel |
| Vulkan | C++17 syntax check against Khronos headers; full SDK and runtime unverified |
| Python | Package/CLI creation, source checks, run, tests, environment selection |
| Cargo 1.98.1 | App/library/workspace, check/build/test/run/fmt/Clippy, extra binary |
| Apple Swift 6.4 | App/library, build/run/test, format/lint, source addition |

SDKs used for validation live in temporary directories; project creation does not download them. The [graphics guide](/docs/graphics/) identifies tested Metal-cpp and Dawn revisions. Missing GPU/context returns 77 and is reported as a skipped CTest, not a passing hardware test.

## Current boundaries

Existing CMake, Python, Cargo, and SwiftPM projects can run native tasks without a Rivet manifest. File moves/removals and explicit module renames operate on registered C/C++ files. They preserve source-list ownership and review snapshots; C++ identifier/include refactoring and adopting arbitrary CMake source lists remain separate work.

Python's Build task checks syntax; Package builds a distribution through installed packaging tools. Native package managers own dependencies and lockfiles. SwiftUI apps, signing, simulators, and previews remain in Xcode. WebGPU support is native; browser deployment is not generated.

Cluster/profiler integration is an explicit argv-hook interface. There is no background job monitor, resource allocator, or automatic remote deployment.
